Overlay patch 0064 (D17): ASYNCHRONOUS JUMBOTRON FRAMEBUFFER READBACK
(`fbrd`; the MK64 analogue of Shipwright 0067 / 2ship 0049, D-086/D-059).

WHY. race_logic_loop (src/main.c) ends EVERY race display list with
FB_WriteFramebufferSliceToCPU (src/racing/framebuffer_effects.c): the frame is
scaled into the 320x240 gN64ResFrameBuffer and `gDPReadFB` copies it to
gPortFramebuffers[sRenderingFramebuffer] as RGBA16 -- the feed of the Luigi
Raceway / Wario Stadium jumbotrons (they draw gPortFramebuffers[prevFrame]).
The interpreter (gfx_read_fb_handler_custom) calls
GfxRenderingAPIMetal::ReadFramebufferToCPU on that custom framebuffer once per
DISPLAY frame (each interpolated replay of the list included). Upstream's
custom-FB branch allocates a fresh MTL::Buffer, encodes the RGBA5551 compute
conversion, commits and waitUntilCompleted()s -- a synchronous GPU round-trip
on the game thread every frame. The 0062 probe put it at 586-591 calls and
0.56-1.6 s per 10 s on the sim (1-2.7 ms per frame), the largest game-thread
category, and it scales with whatever the GPU still has in flight.

WHAT (gSohIos.AsyncFbRead, default 1; 0 = upstream synchronous path).
Same shape as the Ship of Harkinian port async depth readback: submit without waiting,
answer from the newest FINISHED readback, cache the state it rebuilt.
  1. SUBMIT: the conversion dispatch is encoded exactly as upstream, into one
     of three persistent shared result buffers (no per-frame newBuffer), and
     committed; the command buffer is retained in the slot, never waited on.
  2. HARVEST (same call, before serving): every slot whose command buffer has
     completed is retired; the newest completed one is copied into a per-FB
     CPU cache and its slot freed.
  3. SERVE: the cache is copied into the caller's buffer (the interpreter
     byte-swaps it afterwards, unchanged). So the jumbotron shows the frame
     rendered one display frame earlier (two at worst) -- at 60 Hz with 30 Hz
     game frames that is invisible; the game itself already shows the
     PREVIOUS game frame's buffer on the screen.
  - No free slot (GPU three readbacks behind): wait on the oldest one -- a
    bounded stall, counted (`wait`), instead of unbounded growth.
  - The very first call per FB stays on upstream's path (its command buffer
    is on the main queue then; upstream returns zeros and moves the FB to the
    readback queue). Until a readback completes the cache is zeros (black),
    same as upstream's first frame.
  - The ConvertToRgb5a1 PSO was already created once and cached by upstream;
    unchanged. The per-frame MTL::Buffer allocation is what goes away.
  - Screen FB (fb 0) path untouched (already deferred upstream; MK64 does not
    use it).

COUNTERS. SOH_FBASYNC every 10 s: submit, fresh (served the previous call's
readback), old (served an older one: GPU more than a frame behind), wait
(forced oldest-slot waits), empty (nothing finished yet: zeros), inflight
(slots still on the GPU at submit time). The 0062 `fbrd` seam still wraps the
whole call, so fbrd now measures encode + commit + memcpy.

Platform: __IOS__ covers the iPhone and visionOS builds; the macOS build is
unchanged.

Idempotent: if the edits are already applied the generator reverses them
first, so it runs against a vendor tree (0001..0063 applied) with or without
0064. Every edit asserts exactly one match.

REV2 (VR R19 item 4, 2026-10-09): ONE READBACK PER GAME FRAME IN VR.
the user on 1.0.0.17: with the Top rear-view pane on, the Luigi Raceway
jumbotron shows the rear view in its top-left corner; with it off it
"sometimes flickers a REAR view". In VR every interpolated host frame walks
the display list THREE times -- eye 1, eye 2, and (while a pane is shown) the
rear-view pane walk into its own small mirror target (0044 rev3) -- and each
walk ends with the game's G_READFB. rev1 submitted a readback on EVERY walk
(measured on 1.0.0.17's code in a VR Luigi race: ~355 submits/s at ~30 game
frames/s = ~12 per game frame, 2-11 forced waits per 10 s) and served "the
newest finished" one -- which is the pane walk's whenever it finished last: a
REVERSED camera rendered into a target about half the eye's extent, i.e. a
small rear view in the top-left of the 320x240 slice.
Fix: the G_READFB handler (interpreter.cpp, this patch's second file) stamps
the walk identity into gSohFbrdWalk (0044's sSoh3DEye: 0 flat, 1/2 the eyes,
3 the pane; bit 4 = the walk is the game frame's KEY walk, gSohVRWalkT == 1,
0014 rev2). In a VR world frame (gSohVRWorldActive) only eye 1 of the key walk
submits: t = 1 is the game's own state at its own tick -- the exact frame a
flat build's last walk reads -- and eye 1 is the first pass of that walk, so
the readback has the whole rest of the host frame to finish. Every other walk
(eye 2, the pane, the interpolated walks) commits its command buffer exactly
as before and SERVES THE CACHE, so the bytes the game copies (and the
interpreter's in-place byte swap) are unchanged on those walks. A frame
ratio with no key walk cannot freeze the jumbotron: after 8 eye-1 walks
without a submit, eye 1 submits anyway (`fallback`). `gSohVRFbrdGate`
(bridge `vr set fbrdgate 0|1`, ships 1) is the same-binary red control:
0 = rev1 exactly. Flat and 3D-panel frames (gSohVRWorldActive == 0) take
the rev1 path byte for byte.
THE SECOND HALF, found by the instrument, not the brief: with the gate alone
the jumbotron STILL showed the rear view in its top-left corner, now in EYE-1
readbacks (`vr fbrd save` dumps the served bytes). The pane walk's
G_COPYFB copies its mirror target into gReusableFrameBuffer with a dst rect
of its own (smaller) extent -- the top-left -- and the slice's render pass on
the readback queue has no dependency on the main-queue copies, so the next
eye readback samples that corner. So the copy handler (interpreter.cpp) also
runs only on the chosen walk in VR world frames (gReusableFrameBuffer is used
by the jumbotron slice and nothing else); `copy_skips` counts the rest.
Instrument: per-slot walk kind; the cache remembers which walk produced it;
`vr fbrd` (SohIos_FbrdDump) reports cumulative submits and SERVES by
producing walk kind (flat/eye1/eye2/pane/empty), serve-only skips, forced
waits and fallbacks; `vr fbrd reset` zeroes them. SOH_FBASYNC gains
`skip=` and `pane_srv=`.

REV3 (VR R20 item 1, 2026-10-09): THE LEFT-EYE RAINBOW FLASH. the user on
1.0.0.18: "the rainbow flash only happens in my left eye ... several in 40
seconds". Question (a) answered from the code and a ledger: G_READFB commits
the 320x240 slice framebuffer's OWN command buffer (on the readback queue),
never the eye framebuffer's, so no eye pass is split and no publish can fire
early (`eye_cb_splits` counts readbacks aimed at the walk's eye framebuffer:
0). What 0064 DID change is the CPU's lead over the GPU: upstream's readback
waited for the GPU every walk (waitUntilCompleted), which in VR -- no drawable
to throttle on -- was the only thing stopping the game thread from running
whole host frames ahead. The Metal backend writes each walk's vertices into a
pool of THREE shared buffers that rotates once per walk (2-3 walks per host
frame in VR) and never asks whether the GPU is done with one. With the wait
gone (rev1) and then the forced waits gone too (rev2: one submit per game
frame, 0 waits), a walk can memcpy over vertices an earlier queued walk has
not drawn yet. Fix: SohVboNote/SohVboStartWalk (helper block, StartFrame,
DrawTriangles) -- every reader command buffer is retained against its pool
buffer and a walk that would begin writing a buffer with a live reader swaps
in a free one (grown on demand, bounded at 12, bounded wait past that).
`gSohVRVboFix` (bridge `vr set vbofix 0|1`, ships 1) is the same-binary red
control; `vr vbo` the ledger. 3D sessions only (gSoh3DMode): the flat path,
which the drawable throttles, is untouched.
Also rev3: the device A/B arm. `gSohVRJumboFeed` (the shell's persisted
"Jumbotron live feed" row, default On): Off = in a VR world frame the game
emits no jumbotron slice at all (src/racing/framebuffer_effects.c, this
patch's third file) and the jumbotron holds its last image.

--- a/libultraship/src/fast/backends/gfx_metal.cpp
+++ b/libultraship/src/fast/backends/gfx_metal.cpp
@@ -40,6 +40,413 @@
 
 #define ARRAY_COUNT(arr) (int32_t)(sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]))
 
+#ifdef __IOS__
+// SOH_IOS (overlay 0064, D17): asynchronous custom-framebuffer readback (the
+// MK64 jumbotron feed, read every display frame). Submit without waiting,
+// serve the newest finished readback from a CPU cache. See
+// scripts/gen-patch-0064.py.
+#include <chrono>
+namespace {
+constexpr int kSohFbrdSlots = 3;
+struct SohFbrdSlot {
+    MTL::CommandBuffer* cb = nullptr; // retained while in flight
+    MTL::Buffer* buf = nullptr;       // persistent shared result buffer
+    size_t bytes = 0;
+    int fbId = -1;
+    unsigned long long seq = 0;
+    int kind = 0; // rev2: the walk that submitted it (0 flat, 1/2 eyes, 3 pane)
+};
+SohFbrdSlot sSohFbrd[kSohFbrdSlots];
+unsigned long long sSohFbrdSeq = 0;
+std::unordered_map<int, std::vector<uint16_t>> sSohFbrdCache;
+std::unordered_map<int, unsigned long long> sSohFbrdCacheSeq;
+std::unordered_map<int, unsigned long long> sSohFbrdLastSubmit;
+unsigned sSohFbrdStat[8]; // submit fresh old wait empty inflight skip pane_srv
+std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point sSohFbrdWin;
+// rev2 (VR R19 item 4): which walk produced the cached bytes, and cumulative
+// counters for `vr fbrd` (never reset by the 10 s log).
+std::unordered_map<int, int> sSohFbrdCacheKind;
+unsigned long long sSohFbrdKSubmit[4]; // by submitting walk: flat eye1 eye2 pane
+unsigned long long sSohFbrdKServe[5];  // by PRODUCING walk: flat eye1 eye2 pane empty
+unsigned long long sSohFbrdTot[4];     // skip wait fallback calls
+int sSohFbrdEyeSince = 0;              // eye-1 walks since the last VR submit
+unsigned int sSohFbrdWaitsAll = 0;     // forced waits, never reset (SOH_VRGLITCH)
+
+void SohFbrdRetire(SohFbrdSlot& s) {
+    if (s.cb == nullptr) {
+        return;
+    }
+    MTL::CommandBufferStatus st = s.cb->status();
+    if (st == MTL::CommandBufferStatusCompleted) {
+        if (s.seq > sSohFbrdCacheSeq[s.fbId]) {
+            std::vector<uint16_t>& c = sSohFbrdCache[s.fbId];
+            c.resize(s.bytes / sizeof(uint16_t));
+            memcpy(c.data(), s.buf->contents(), s.bytes);
+            sSohFbrdCacheSeq[s.fbId] = s.seq;
+            sSohFbrdCacheKind[s.fbId] = s.kind;
+        }
+        s.cb->release();
+        s.cb = nullptr;
+    } else if (st == MTL::CommandBufferStatusError) {
+        s.cb->release();
+        s.cb = nullptr;
+    }
+}
+
+void SohFbrdLog() {
+    auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
+    if (sSohFbrdWin.time_since_epoch().count() == 0) {
+        sSohFbrdWin = now;
+        return;
+    }
+    double el = std::chrono::duration<double>(now - sSohFbrdWin).count();
+    if (el < 10.0) {
+        return;
+    }
+    SPDLOG_INFO("SOH_FBASYNC el={:.1f} submit={} fresh={} old={} wait={} empty={} inflight={} skip={} pane_srv={}",
+                el, sSohFbrdStat[0], sSohFbrdStat[1], sSohFbrdStat[2], sSohFbrdStat[3], sSohFbrdStat[4],
+                sSohFbrdStat[5], sSohFbrdStat[6], sSohFbrdStat[7]);
+    memset(sSohFbrdStat, 0, sizeof(sSohFbrdStat));
+    sSohFbrdWin = now;
+}
+
+// Harvest finished slots and return a free one sized for `bytes` (waits on
+// the oldest in-flight slot only when all are busy).
+SohFbrdSlot* SohFbrdAcquire(MTL::Device* dev, int fbId, size_t bytes) {
+    SohFbrdLog();
+    SohFbrdSlot* oldest = nullptr;
+    for (auto& s : sSohFbrd) {
+        SohFbrdRetire(s);
+        if (s.cb != nullptr) {
+            sSohFbrdStat[5]++;
+            if (oldest == nullptr || s.seq < oldest->seq) {
+                oldest = &s;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    SohFbrdSlot* slot = nullptr;
+    for (auto& s : sSohFbrd) {
+        if (s.cb == nullptr) {
+            slot = &s;
+            break;
+        }
+    }
+    if (slot == nullptr) {
+        sSohFbrdStat[3]++;
+        sSohFbrdTot[1]++;
+        sSohFbrdWaitsAll++;
+        oldest->cb->waitUntilCompleted();
+        SohFbrdRetire(*oldest);
+        if (oldest->cb != nullptr) { // still not retired (unexpected status): drop it
+            oldest->cb->release();
+            oldest->cb = nullptr;
+        }
+        slot = oldest;
+    }
+    if (slot->buf == nullptr || slot->buf->length() < bytes) {
+        if (slot->buf != nullptr) {
+            slot->buf->release();
+        }
+        slot->buf = dev->newBuffer(bytes, MTL::ResourceStorageModeShared);
+    }
+    slot->bytes = bytes;
+    slot->fbId = fbId;
+    return slot;
+}
+
+// rev2: retire every finished slot without taking one (the serve-only walks).
+void SohFbrdHarvest() {
+    SohFbrdLog();
+    for (auto& s : sSohFbrd) {
+        SohFbrdRetire(s);
+    }
+}
+
+void SohFbrdServe(int fbId, uint16_t* out, size_t count, unsigned long long prevSubmit) {
+    std::vector<uint16_t>& c = sSohFbrdCache[fbId];
+    if (c.size() != count) {
+        sSohFbrdStat[4]++;
+        sSohFbrdKServe[4]++;
+        memset(out, 0, count * sizeof(uint16_t));
+        return;
+    }
+    {
+        const int k = sSohFbrdCacheKind[fbId];
+        sSohFbrdKServe[(k >= 0 && k <= 3) ? k : 0]++;
+        if (k == 3) {
+            sSohFbrdStat[7]++;
+        }
+    }
+    // fresh = the readback submitted by the previous call for this FB
+    sSohFbrdStat[sSohFbrdCacheSeq[fbId] >= prevSubmit ? 1 : 2]++;
+    memcpy(out, c.data(), count * sizeof(uint16_t));
+}
+
+// rev3 (VR R20 item 1): THE VERTEX POOL'S GPU LIFETIME. DrawTriangles memcpy's
+// every walk's vertices into mVertexBufferPool[idx] (three shared buffers,
+// upstream), and the index advances once per EndFrame / EndFrameOffscreen --
+// i.e. once per WALK in VR (eye 1, eye 2, the pane). Upstream relies on the
+// drawable (three in flight) to stop the CPU from rewriting a buffer the GPU
+// is still reading; the VR path never acquires a drawable, and the eye-slot
+// gate lets two whole host frames (4-6 walks) be in flight against a pool of
+// three. So a walk can memcpy over vertices an earlier, still-queued walk is
+// about to draw: garbage triangles in THAT walk's eye. Every reader command
+// buffer is retained against the buffer it read (SohVboNote, per draw,
+// deduplicated) and a walk that would start writing a buffer with a reader
+// not yet COMPLETED swaps in a free buffer instead (growing the set, bounded),
+// so the write never lands under the GPU. Measured in every arm (trap D48):
+// `busy` = the walk's pool buffer still had a live reader when the walk began;
+// `write_busy` = the walk wrote into one anyway (the hazard; 0 is green).
+constexpr int kSohVboMax = 12; // buffers ever tracked (3 upstream + spares)
+constexpr int kSohVboCbs = 32; // live readers per buffer
+struct SohVboTrack {
+    MTL::Buffer* buf = nullptr;
+    MTL::CommandBuffer* cbs[kSohVboCbs] = {};
+    int kind[kSohVboCbs] = {}; // the walk kind that encoded each reader
+    int ncb = 0;
+};
+SohVboTrack sSohVbo[kSohVboMax];
+int sSohVboN = 0;
+unsigned long long sSohVboWalks[4];     // by walk kind: flat/panel, eye 1, eye 2, pane
+unsigned long long sSohVboBusy[4];      // pool buffer still read by the GPU at walk start
+unsigned long long sSohVboWriteBusy[4]; // ... and the walk wrote into it anyway
+unsigned long long sSohVboSwaps = 0, sSohVboAllocs = 0, sSohVboWaits = 0, sSohVboOverflow = 0;
+unsigned long long sSohVboWriteBusyAll = 0; // never reset (SOH_VRGLITCH)
+unsigned long long sSohVboBusyAll = 0;      // never reset (SOH_VRGLITCH)
+unsigned long long sSohVboVictim[4];        // live readers under a write, by THEIR walk kind
+
+SohVboTrack* SohVboFind(MTL::Buffer* b, bool add) {
+    for (int i = 0; i < sSohVboN; i++) {
+        if (sSohVbo[i].buf == b) {
+            return &sSohVbo[i];
+        }
+    }
+    if (!add || b == nullptr || sSohVboN >= kSohVboMax) {
+        return nullptr;
+    }
+    sSohVbo[sSohVboN].buf = b;
+    return &sSohVbo[sSohVboN++];
+}
+
+// Drop the readers the GPU has finished with; true while one may still read
+// the buffer. A reader that was never committed reads as busy (conservative).
+bool SohVboBusy(SohVboTrack* t) {
+    int k = 0;
+    for (int i = 0; i < t->ncb; i++) {
+        MTL::CommandBufferStatus st = t->cbs[i]->status();
+        if (st == MTL::CommandBufferStatusCompleted || st == MTL::CommandBufferStatusError) {
+            t->cbs[i]->release();
+        } else {
+            t->kind[k] = t->kind[i];
+            t->cbs[k++] = t->cbs[i];
+        }
+    }
+    for (int i = k; i < t->ncb; i++) {
+        t->cbs[i] = nullptr;
+    }
+    t->ncb = k;
+    return k > 0;
+}
+} // namespace
+
+extern "C" {
+extern volatile int gSoh3DMode;
+volatile int gSohVRVboFix = 1; // rev3: 1 = ship; 0 = 1.0.0.18 (bridge `vr set vbofix`)
+int SohIos_WalkEye(void);      // interpreter.cpp (rev3): 0044's sSoh3DEye
+}
+
+namespace {
+// Per draw, 3D sessions only: `cb` reads `b`.
+void SohVboNote(MTL::Buffer* b, MTL::CommandBuffer* cb) {
+    if (gSoh3DMode == 0 || cb == nullptr) {
+        return;
+    }
+    SohVboTrack* t = SohVboFind(b, true);
+    if (t == nullptr) {
+        sSohVboOverflow++;
+        return;
+    }
+    for (int i = t->ncb - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
+        if (t->cbs[i] == cb) {
+            return;
+        }
+    }
+    if (t->ncb >= kSohVboCbs) {
+        SohVboBusy(t);
+        if (t->ncb >= kSohVboCbs) {
+            sSohVboOverflow++;
+            return;
+        }
+    }
+    const int sohEye = SohIos_WalkEye();
+    t->kind[t->ncb] = (sohEye >= 0 && sohEye <= 3) ? sohEye : 0;
+    t->cbs[t->ncb++] = cb->retain();
+}
+
+// The head of every walk (backend StartFrame, offset already 0): the walk is
+// about to write pool[idx] from byte 0.
+void SohVboStartWalk(MTL::Device* dev, MTL::Buffer** pool, int idx, int poolN) {
+    if (gSoh3DMode == 0) {
+        return;
+    }
+    const int sohEye = SohIos_WalkEye();
+    const int ek = (sohEye >= 0 && sohEye <= 3) ? sohEye : 0;
+    sSohVboWalks[ek]++;
+    SohVboTrack* t = SohVboFind(pool[idx], true);
+    const bool busy = t != nullptr && SohVboBusy(t);
+    if (busy) {
+        sSohVboBusy[ek]++;
+        sSohVboBusyAll++;
+    }
+    if (busy && gSohVRVboFix != 0) {
+        SohVboTrack* pick = nullptr;
+        for (int i = 0; i < sSohVboN && pick == nullptr; i++) {
+            SohVboTrack* c = &sSohVbo[i];
+            bool inPool = false;
+            for (int j = 0; j < poolN; j++) {
+                inPool = inPool || pool[j] == c->buf;
+            }
+            if (!inPool && !SohVboBusy(c)) {
+                pick = c;
+            }
+        }
+        if (pick == nullptr && sSohVboN < kSohVboMax) {
+            MTL::Buffer* nb = dev->newBuffer(pool[idx]->length(), MTL::ResourceStorageModeShared);
+            if (nb != nullptr) {
+                pick = SohVboFind(nb, true);
+                sSohVboAllocs++;
+            }
+        }
+        if (pick != nullptr) {
+            pool[idx] = pick->buf;
+            sSohVboSwaps++;
+        } else {
+            // every spare is in flight too: wait for this one, bounded (trap C1)
+            sSohVboWaits++;
+            for (int s = 0; s < 100 && SohVboBusy(t); s++) {
+                struct timespec sohNap = { 0, 500000 };
+                nanosleep(&sohNap, nullptr);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    SohVboTrack* f = SohVboFind(pool[idx], false);
+    if (f != nullptr && SohVboBusy(f)) {
+        sSohVboWriteBusy[ek]++;
+        sSohVboWriteBusyAll++;
+        for (int i = 0; i < f->ncb; i++) {
+            sSohVboVictim[f->kind[i]]++; // whose vertices this walk is about to overwrite
+        }
+    }
+}
+} // namespace
+
+// rev2 (VR R19 item 4): the walk identity, stamped by the G_READFB handler
+// (interpreter.cpp): 0044's sSoh3DEye in bits 0-3, 0x10 = the key walk (t = 1).
+// gSohVRFbrdGate: 1 = in a VR world frame only eye 1 of the key walk submits;
+// 0 = rev1 exactly (every walk submits) -- the same-binary red control.
+extern "C" {
+volatile int gSohFbrdWalk = 0;
+volatile int gSohVRFbrdGate = 1;
+volatile unsigned int gSohVRFbrdCopySkips = 0; // copies skipped (interpreter.cpp)
+extern volatile int gSohVRWorldActive;
+
+static char sSohFbrdLine[512];
+const char* SohIos_FbrdDump(void) {
+    snprintf(sSohFbrdLine, sizeof(sSohFbrdLine),
+             "fbrdgate=%d calls=%llu submit_flat=%llu submit_eye1=%llu submit_eye2=%llu submit_pane=%llu "
+             "serve_flat=%llu serve_eye1=%llu serve_eye2=%llu serve_pane=%llu serve_empty=%llu "
+             "skip=%llu wait=%llu fallback=%llu copy_skips=%u",
+             gSohVRFbrdGate, sSohFbrdTot[3], sSohFbrdKSubmit[0], sSohFbrdKSubmit[1], sSohFbrdKSubmit[2],
+             sSohFbrdKSubmit[3], sSohFbrdKServe[0], sSohFbrdKServe[1], sSohFbrdKServe[2], sSohFbrdKServe[3],
+             sSohFbrdKServe[4], sSohFbrdTot[0], sSohFbrdTot[1], sSohFbrdTot[2], gSohVRFbrdCopySkips);
+    return sSohFbrdLine;
+}
+// rev2: write the cached readback (the bytes the jumbotron is served, RGBA5551,
+// native order, before the interpreter's byte swap) to `path`; returns the
+// producing walk kind (0 flat, 1/2 eyes, 3 pane) or -1. Bridge `vr fbrd save`.
+int SohIos_FbrdSave(const char* path, int* w_out) {
+    for (auto& e : sSohFbrdCache) {
+        if (e.second.empty()) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        FILE* f = fopen(path, "wb");
+        if (f == nullptr) {
+            return -1;
+        }
+        fwrite(e.second.data(), sizeof(uint16_t), e.second.size(), f);
+        fclose(f);
+        if (w_out != nullptr) {
+            *w_out = (int)e.second.size();
+        }
+        return sSohFbrdCacheKind[e.first];
+    }
+    return -1;
+}
+// rev2 (R19 item 5): the device glitch line's two hooks -- the never-reset
+// forced-wait count, and SohIos_SpdLogLine (NOT the shell's SohIos_LogLine,
+// which is the crash blackbox) for the Objective-C shell to write one line
+// into the same spdlog file SOH_PERF / SOH_FBASYNC go to (Documents/logs).
+unsigned int SohIos_FbrdWaitsAll(void) {
+    return sSohFbrdWaitsAll;
